A||||10||Kevin Larke||Brake Problem||||9||
Z||000000||Kevin Larke||04-10-2000||08:08 PM||||I have an '85 4 cyl that has mainly been in storage for 9 years. Had 73,000 miles in 4/91, now has 76,250. I put insurance on it and bought a 14 day registration. My everyday pickup is in the body shop getting a door painted under warranty. Anyway, here's my brake problem.....<P>I noticed yesterday that it was pulling to the left a little at times, but not always. After a few episodes of this, I realized that the left front brake wasn't releasing.<BR>The rotor and wheel were good and hot when I made it home. I could smell it. I missed work today so I could take care of the problem. I suspected a sticking caliper. I bought a new set of pads, two caliper seal kits, brake fluid, high temp silicone brake grease, etc.  I took everything apart and cleaned all the parts. I replaced the o-ring (actually has a rectangular cross section) and the piston seal boot on both calipers. I cleaned and lubed the sleeves for the mounting bolts so they would move freely. None of the parts looked that bad. I put everything back together, bled the fronts, and VOILA! SAME PROBLEM! If I have someone push the brakes while I spin the wheels, the left front doesn't release. I tried to drive it and it pulled to the left. It was good and hot when I got home. I can squeeze it with a c-clamp to make it lose it's grip, but it happens again when they're pushed.<P>Do I need to buy rebuilt calipers? Both of the old ones look OK to me, but what else could it be? I thought it might be that brass valve near the master cylinder, but wouldn't both fronts do it if that was causing it?<P>Please help me out. I'll have to miss work again if I can't fix this in the morning.<BR>Thanks again in advance. ||||reg||9
Z||000001||batboy||04-10-2000||08:54 PM||||Sounds like the caliper piston is hanging up on a groove or some rust pits. This happens with cars that have been parked a long time. Did you hone out the cylinder? I'm guessing not, probably need a complete rebuild kit rather than just a seal kit. I never mess with rebuilding calipers myself. I'd replace both front calipers as a pair and the flexible brake hoses too. Brakes are important, especially the front ones.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 04-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||daved||04-10-2000||09:11 PM||||Haynes mentions 4 reasons for dragging brakes on page 26, Item 66.<BR>1 Incorrect adjustment of brake light switch and/or cruise control vacuum release valve assy. (Keeps brake pedal from fully returning)<P>2 Master cylinder pistons not returning correctly<P>3 Restricted brake line or hoses<P>4 Incorrect parking brake adjustment<P>Can't be 4, and unlikely to be 1 (unless you know some work was done in this area)<P>2 would be difficult to diagnose...but in any case it would most likely effect both front brakes equally.<P>Ahha! that leaves 3 which given the posts on the Forum, about the collapse of flex brake hoses, would be my bet!<P>You can exert many hundreds of pounds of pressure when applying the brakes, but only the runout of the rotor from dead true pushes the pads away--far enough to prevent dragging--but close enough to keep them clean.  therefore, any restriction in the return of hydraulic fluid to the master cylinder...even a few pounds...will cause the brake to drag.<P>I suggest you replace both front hydraulic flex hoses (and consider the rear ones too!)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Kevin Larke||04-10-2000||11:20 PM||||Thanks a lot for the responses. The caliper body is cast aluminum so it can't really have rust pits. I didn't see any corrosion or wear in the piston bores. I don't think they need honing, they look good as new. The fluid was light brown with some tiny black particles, but after using brake cleaner it looked shiny and new. The piston seems to be made of some kind of black plastic with a steel cap that touches the pad. The steel cap was a little rusty, but I cleaned it and it doesn't go into the bore anyway.<P>Are the flexible brake lines known for going bad? These look OK from the outside, but they might be colapsed inside or something. I really don't think the caliper is the problem. Are the hoses available at a store like Autozone or do I need to go to a Pontiac dealer? The hose was easy to remove at the caliper, but how does it come off at the body? I guess I'll try the hoses next. If that doesn't do it, I'll get a pair of rebuilt calipers. I really need this thing to get to work this week. Now I'm starting to remember why I bought a new truck 9 years ago. I'll have to find a Nissan web site so I can post a message about the 236,000 trouble free miles I've had with that thing. I can't talk about it around here (I live in "GM Land" where saying anything good about a foreign vehicle will get you a good beating).||||reg||1
Z||000004||fieroshop||04-10-2000||11:31 PM||||You can guess all you want and until such time as you replace the calyper you will have the same results. If you had bad brake hoses the brake petal would feel spongy. That is why people replace the brake hoses because they can nl longer hold pressure and expand when the brake is applied. So no you do not have brake hose problems.||||reg||1
Z||000005||sully5||04-11-2000||08:14 AM||||You might also check the tabs where the caliper slides to make sure that these are good and clean.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||04-11-2000||08:58 AM||||I'm with Batboy, Kevin.  You don't want to take chances with brakes!  Rebuilt calipers for the 84-87's are pretty reasonable - I'd buy the two fronts and replace all four brake hoses at the same time.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||04-11-2000||08:41 PM||||plastic pistons are known for going to hell. replace them. Likely one has gone out of round enough to bind.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Kevin Larke||04-11-2000||11:21 PM||||I put a new pair of rebuilt calipers on the front today. They were only $24.51 + $12 core at a Carquest store. They looked exactly like the originals, only clean and shiny. It looks like the pistons were used. The steel caps that touch the pads were pitted from rust, with a messy silver paint job. Maybe the plastic piston is new (I hope). The good news is, it solved the problem! It feels great to have the it solved. The plastic pistons must "swell" or go out of round with age. <P>My 10 year old son pushed the brake pedal while I bled the brakes. He was proud of himself for being able to help. I told him I wouldn't have been able to do it without him. That was true because nobody else was home. Another advantage to working on your own cars. Your kids learn something and share the feeling of accomplishment.<P>Thanks to everyone who posted responses. This is going to be a great help as I save my Fiero til it's a classic. I still want to drive it in parades when my hair is as white as the paint. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||04-12-2000||05:47 PM||||To answer the earlier question: Do the flexible brake hose go bad? The answer is yes they do. Sometimes they just slowly deteriorate on the inside which cause it to bulge at the weak spot. This will usually cause brake darting, where it swerve for a second to one side before the pressure equalizes.<P>That's why I didn't think it was only a hose, because he didn't mention the darting. Also, it sounded like he definitely had a caliper sticking from his description.<P>I did have a hose that got completely blocked once before. Another thing that happens is that the inside of the hold down clamp, in the middle of the hose, sometimes gets rusty which swells and constricts the hose.||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||04-12-2000||09:17 PM||||Kevin Larke, I had the same problem as you and like you I rebuilt the calipers without honing the bore and my brake still dragged after I put it back together. I ended up giving in and getting a rebuilt caliper for $25 from the parts store. I wish I could have saved myself the trouble and frustration as you must feel and just got the rebuilt caliper in the first place.  <P>You should just get a rebuilt one and make sure the slides are lubed with caliper grease or silicone grease. Don’t use regular wheel bearing grease or the O-rings will swell up and cause the calipers to stick. You don’t want that to happen after spending $25 a caliper.<P>Good luke.<P>||||reg||1
